The code follows the slope instead of checking both neighbors. When `arr[middle]` is smaller than the next value, the array is going upward, so the peak must be on the right. When it is larger, the peak can be at `middle` or to its left.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int findPeakElement(vector<int> &arr) {
        int left = 0;
        int right = static_cast<int>(arr.size()) - 1;

        while (left < right) {
            int middle = left + (right - left) / 2;

            if (arr[middle] < arr[middle + 1]) {
                left = middle + 1;
            } else {
                right = middle;
            }
        }

        return left;
    }
};
```

A quick run with `[1, 2, 1, 3, 5, 6, 4]` starts at the middle value `3`. Since `3 < 5`, the left bound moves right. The next checks move toward index `5`, where value `6` is a peak.

The loop also handles length `1`, because both bounds already start at `0`. The input vector stays unchanged.

Time complexity: `O(log n)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

This keeps two indexes, one near each end, and checks both positions before moving inward. It is still a linear method, but it can find a peak near either side without waiting for a full left-to-right scan.

```csharp
public class Solution {
    public int FindPeakElement(List<int> arr) {
        int left = 0;
        int right = arr.Count - 1;

        while (left <= right) {
            if (IsPeak(arr, left)) {
                return left;
            }

            if (right != left && IsPeak(arr, right)) {
                return right;
            }

            left++;
            right--;
        }

        return -1;
    }

    private bool IsPeak(List<int> arr, int index) {
        bool largerThanLeft = index == 0 || arr[index] > arr[index - 1];
        bool largerThanRight = index == arr.Count - 1 || arr[index] > arr[index + 1];
        return largerThanLeft && largerThanRight;
    }
}
```

With `[1, 2, 3, 1]`, the first pass checks indexes `0` and `3`. The next pass checks `1` and `2`, and index `2` succeeds.

The boundary checks cover an array of one item and peaks at the first or last index. The list is only read, not changed.

Time complexity: `O(n)` overall, since each position is checked at most once.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

The array does not need to be sorted for binary search here. At the middle index, compare the value with its right neighbor. If the right side is higher, a peak must be somewhere to the right. Otherwise, the middle index can still be a peak, so keep it in the left part.

```java
class Solution {
    public int findPeakElement(int[] arr) {
        return search(arr, 0, arr.length - 1);
    }

    private int search(int[] arr, int left, int right) {
        if (left == right) {
            return left;
        }

        int middle = left + (right - left) / 2;

        if (arr[middle] < arr[middle + 1]) {
            return search(arr, middle + 1, right);
        }

        return search(arr, left, middle);
    }
}
```

For an array with one value, the first call has equal bounds and returns `0`. A strictly increasing array ends at its last index, while a decreasing array keeps the search on the left.

The recursive calls do not modify the input array. The recursion uses stack space.

Time complexity: `O(log n)`.
Space complexity: `O(log n)` because of the recursive calls.

There is a short observation that avoids neighbor handling. A maximum value in the whole array must be a peak because it cannot be smaller than either neighbor. Since neighboring values are different, the maximum is strictly greater than the values beside it when those neighbors exist.

```java
class Solution {
    public int findPeakElement(int[] arr) {
        int bestIndex = 0;

        for (int index = 1; index < arr.length; index++) {
            if (arr[index] > arr[bestIndex]) {
                bestIndex = index;
            }
        }

        return bestIndex;
    }
}
```

For `[4, 1, 7, 3]`, the largest value is `7`, so index `2` is returned. This also works for a one-element array and for a peak at either end.

The trade-off is that it scans the whole array even when an earlier local peak could have been returned. It only reads the input.

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

